<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Democratic Party (Uganda)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Democratic Party is a Ugandan political party founded in 1954, which drew much of its support from Catholics, particularly in Buganda. Its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedicto-kiwanuka">Benedicto Kiwanuka</a> headed the government under internal self-government in 1961–1962, but it lost the pre-independence election of 1962 to the alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-peoples-congress">Uganda People's Congress</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-yekka">Kabaka Yekka</a>. It was the main opposition party in the disputed election of 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda.jpg" alt="Democratic Party (Uganda)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Democratic Party · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Democratic_Party_of_Uganda.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Milton Obote, leader of the Uganda People’s Congress, became prime minister through an alliance with the Buganda royalist movement Kabaka Yekka, defeating the <b>Democratic Party</b> of Benedicto Kiwanuka, who had led the government during the brief period of internal self-government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Parties of the name included the General German Workers’ Association and the Social Democratic Workers’ Party, which merged to form the Social <b>Democratic Party</b> of Germany, the Social Democratic Federation in Britain and the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-democracy">Social democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Encouraged by Pete Seeger, whose group the Weavers had recorded an unreleased version and who was then blacklisted, the Toronto folk group the Travellers rewrote it with Canadian places in about 1956; delegates sang it at the founding convention of the New <b>Democratic Party</b> in 1961.</blockquote><a href="/songs/this-land-is-your-land">This Land Is Your Land</a></li><li><blockquote>In September 1945 the Azerbaijan <b>Democratic Party</b> was founded in Tabriz under Ja'far Pishevari, a veteran communist, with Soviet encouragement.</blockquote><a href="/state/azerbaijan-peoples-government">Azerbaijan People's Government</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmed Sekou Toure and his <b>Democratic Party</b> of Guinea campaigned against the 1958 constitution, and on 28 September 1958 Guinea voted no. It became independent on 2 October 1958, and France withdrew its staff and aid abruptly.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-guinea">French Guinea</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Soviet occupation of northern Iran, Kurdish nationalists in Mahabad formed the Komala society and then the Kurdistan <b>Democratic Party</b> under Qazi Muhammad, a respected judge and religious leader.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mahabad">Republic of Mahabad</a></li></ul></section>
